WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.408
In embedded systems, availability is often
part of security.

2
00:00:03.488 --> 00:00:06.340
A device that misses timing, blocks a
critical control path,

3
00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.223
ignores backpressure, or becomes stuck in
lower-value work can fail in ways that

4
00:00:10.303 --> 00:00:12.540
matter to safety, reliability, and user
trust.

5
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.255
RTIC priority design should reflect what
must happen quickly, what may wait,

6
00:00:16.335 --> 00:00:18.404
and what work should be deferred or
bounded.

7
00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:22.343
High priority should not become a place
for unlimited parsing, logging, storage,

8
00:00:22.423 --> 00:00:23.541
or communication work.

9
00:00:23.621 --> 00:00:26.977
Priority expresses operational intent, so
it deserves design review.

10
00:00:27.057 --> 00:00:31.027
Long-running work can starve
lower-priority tasks or delay critical maintenance.

11
00:00:31.107 --> 00:00:32.617
Even if the code is memory-safe,

12
00:00:32.697 --> 00:00:36.109
a task that monopolizes execution can
interfere with watchdog service,

13
00:00:36.189 --> 00:00:39.041
command handling, sensor processing, or
recovery actions.

14
00:00:39.121 --> 00:00:42.197
Timing risks are not always visible from
functional tests alone.

15
00:00:42.277 --> 00:00:46.024
Defensive design uses bounded work, clear
scheduling assumptions, timeouts,

16
00:00:46.104 --> 00:00:48.005
watchdog thinking, and recovery paths.

17
00:00:48.085 --> 00:00:49.595
It asks what happens under load,

18
00:00:49.675 --> 00:00:52.247
what happens when input arrives faster
than expected,

19
00:00:52.327 --> 00:00:55.180
and what happens when hardware responds
slowly or not at all.

20
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.224
Timing assumptions should be written in
reviewable language.

21
00:00:58.304 --> 00:01:01.547
If a task must finish quickly, say what
quickly means for the product.

22
00:01:01.627 --> 00:01:04.479
If work is deferred, state what queue or
signal limits apply.

23
00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:06.125
If a watchdog is part of recovery,

24
00:01:06.205 --> 00:01:10.176
define what conditions it is expected to
catch and what evidence should be available

25
00:01:10.256 --> 00:01:11.039
after recovery.

26
00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:13.467
A useful review question is not only, can
this run,

27
00:01:13.547 --> 00:01:15.952
but can this run safely under degraded
conditions.

28
00:01:16.032 --> 00:01:18.940
RTIC gives structure for priority and
scheduling decisions.

29
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.711
Security comes from using that structure
to make timing behavior measurable,

30
00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:25.140
bounded, and aligned with product
requirements.
